        "payload": {
          "readable": "{\"evidence_hashes\":[\"078bdf7993d2c61410a60a81abb08b4d7b766c182e3a2b1e41b8a4196e864746\",\"26367b2c906eff16a2999534ad3dd37784804556637d3d76da8e326eec1597b1\"],\"explanation\":\"The need document explicitly rejects PVC, and the supply document identifies the material as PVC. Both documents independently confirm this incompatibility: the need states 'reject PVC' and documents that PVC releases dioxins and is not safe to process, while the supply document states 'PVC is toxic and we do not work with it' and 'don't use it' with Precious Plastic machines. This is a documented contradiction, not merely an absence of information. The 400g exchange quantity is above the 1g minimum, so quantity is not the disqualifying factor; the material identity itself is the disqualifying factor.\",\"quotes\":{\"need\":\"y toxic compound that has been shown to increase cancer, reproductive problems and immune system damage. At Precious Plastic, we don\\u2019t work with PVC.\\n\\nIf you do need to burn a small piece of plastic to identify its type, then do so taking the proper precautions: wearing a mask and beside a proper fume filtration system. The most important thing about working with plastic: <b>You can melt it, you just can\\u2019t burn it.</b> And that\\u2019s an important difference.\\n\\n##  Volatile Organic\",\"supply\":\"cking film<br>\\n<b>Best ways to use with PP machines:</b> HDPE works very similar to PP, low melting temperature and easy to mould. Great material to use!\\n\\n<img style=\\\"margin-left: 0; margin-top: 50px\\\" src=\\\"../assets/plastic/type-pvc.svg\\\" width=\\\"80\\\"/>\\n\\n### PVC (3): Polyvinyl chloride\\nPVC is toxic and we do not work with it. It is most commonly found in plumbing pipes and releases chloride when heated up - we do not recommend using with Precious Plastic machines!\\n\\n<b> Propertie\"},\"reason\":\"semantic_review\",\"verdict\":\"no_fit\"}"
